  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\nQuiet weather persists today with the presence of high pressure.\nMain feature to monitor tonight will be fog development, primarily \nin portions of the Scioto Valley and north-central KY. These \nlocations are where mid level clouds will be less disruptive to fog \ndevelopment. \n\nA cold front will continue sink southward from the Great Lakes region\ntomorrow. The combination of the frontal boundary and a series of \nshortwaves rippling through will result in scattered shower/storm \ndevelopment. Initial storm development is expected near the I-70 \ncorridor, primarily late afternoon/evening. Additional convection \nwill spawn farther south and continue overnight. The parameter space\nfor severe potential is not overly impressive. ML tools also not \nshowing much (if any) severe probs in our CWA. However, there are \nsome signals that indicate an isolated flood risk exists given the \nhigh PWATs near 1.75\", deeply saturated sounding profiles and \nefficient, warm rain processes. CAMs struggling to hone in on exact \nareas of concern, but it would appear that locations near I-70 would \nprimarily have these heavy rain concerns during the afternoon and \nevening, while locations farther south would be more of an overnight \nthreat. Would likely need multiple rounds of storms to induce a hydro\nconcern, but cannot rule out this potential. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\n\nModel guidance has been fairly consistent in a signal for shower and\nthunderstorm activity on Saturday. Latest timing would suggest that \nthe bulk of the convective activity will be during the daytime hours.\nThe cold front that is helping to initialize this activity appears \nto be fairly progressive, so this should help limit the threat for \nheavy rain. Still a bit early to delve into the severe parameter \ndetails, but this system is worth monitoring as plenty of instability\nwill be around on Saturday.",
